{"step_index":66,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T13:23:11Z","input_tokens":3665,"cache_read_tokens":63218,"output_tokens":1973,"thinking":"The iOS app currently displays reactions solely within the text chat, unlike the desktop version, which duplicates them in the live transcription view. The goal is to ensure parity, making reactions visible in the live transcription text view on iOS.\n\n\nExamining the desktop application reveals reactions are displayed within both the text chat and the live transcription view. The iOS app currently only shows reactions in the text chat. To achieve feature parity, the iOS app must also render reactions within its live transcription view. This suggests a need to integrate reaction display logic within the corresponding iOS view, similar to the desktop implementation.\n\n\nThe desktop implementation generates `liveTextRows` by interleaving transcript lines and reaction events, sorting them chronologically. The key is combining the `transcript` (spoken lines) and `chat.reactionHistory.events` into a single, time-ordered sequence. The iOS implementation likely needs to adopt a similar approach to integrate reactions into the live transcription view.\n\n\nThe iOS app employs a segmented control with tabs for Chat, Live Text, and Catch Up. Within `ConversationPanelViewController.swift`, the `renderTranscript()` function seems responsible for updating the live text view, but needs to incorporate reaction events to match the desktop behavior. The goal now is to determine how reaction events can be integrated into the transcription display logic within this function, mirroring the desktop approach of combining transcript lines and reaction events.\n\n\n`renderTranscript()` appears responsible for updating the live text view, but the function does not incorporate reaction events. It calls `liveTranscript()` and `transcriptLines(...)`, which seem to handle formatting the transcript content. Further investigation of these functions is needed to understand how to integrate reaction data. The `transcriptLines()` function likely needs modification to include reaction-related attributes within the attributed string.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"git grep -n \"catchUp.observe\" RockNRoll/","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Find catchUp.observe calls"}}]}
